notes for running gazebo / ros

start by setting turtlebot model
export TURTLEBOT3_MODEL=waffle_pi

open gazebo sim
ros2 launch <package name> <package script py>

open new terminal
launch SLAM
ros2 launch slam_toolbox online_async_launch.py

open new terminal
set turtlebot model again, export...
export TURTLEBOT3_MODEL=waffle_pi
open rvis2:
ros2 run rviz2 rviz2

In RViz, add the \map topic (Add → By topic → Map).
- means you're subscribing to the 'map' publications in RViz

to move bot
open new terminal
export TURTLEBOT3_MODEL=waffle_pi
ros2 run turtlebot3_teleop teleop_keyboard

to make new package
cd into workspace/src
ros2 pkg create --build-type ament_python waypoint_commander --dependencies rclpy nav2_msgs geometry_msgs

Note : to add other message types and dependencies can be found https://docs.ros2.org/foxy/api/
then:
cd ../src/<package name>/<package name> 
create new python:
touch <script name>.py

In python script:
import relevant files:
from nav2_msgs import msg
import rclpy
from rclpy.node import Node
from nav2_msgs.msg import BehaviorTreeLog (example)
from geometry_msgs.msg import PoseStamped (example)

main section:
def main(args=None):
    rclpy.init(args=args)

    # Create a new node
    <var name> = <node class name>()
    # Execute the node
    rclpy.spin(<var name>)


if __name__ == '__main__':
    main()
    
    
Node class:
create node that subscribes to the information you seek, publishes to a topic that rviz can access

Giving ROS access:
in top level directory of <package name>, in setup.py, inside square brackets of 'console_scripts':
'<script name (no py)> = <package name>.<scriptname>:main'

in terminal, in workspace folder, build package:
colcon build --symlink-install --packages-select <package name>

source install/setup.bash

run node (after gazebo/rvis are set up):
ros2 run <package name> <package script>


for looking at what is being published
ros2 topic hz /camera/image_raw
for looking at k matrix and resolution
ros2 topic echo /camera/camera_info --once 
for looking directly at camera feed
ros2 run rqt_image_view rqt_image_view

for running marker_images:
ros2 bag play marker_run --loop --clock
ros2 run rviz2 rviz2 --ros-args -p use_sim_time:=true
ros2 run mapping_pkg camera_reader --ros-args -p use_sim_time:=true